POI##MATS#DUSTY
#ECONOMY#BITER#
#SENATE#PANTRY#
###EYELASHES###


A1. If this hadn't been going around in circles, there would have been no depression. ~ WHIRLPOOL
A9. We hope this plan works out all right. ~ SCHEME
A11. Takes advantage of. ~ AVAILS
A14. What grandmother liked to do to doll up. ~ PRIMP
A15. A German crook that has recently returned to this country. ~ PRETZEL
A16. These were brushed off as the train was rounding a curve, entering the city. ~ COATS
A17. While hanging around a garden, these got into a row with the Melons. ~ BEANS
A18. What Empress Josephine used to call the Emperor. ~ NAP
A20. 80% booze. ~ OOZE
A21. These were used for an economy drive. ~ FORDS
A22. Where a cockney got stung. ~ IVE
A23. Someone is always picking on this poor thing. ~ UKE
A24. This has never been averse to anyone. ~ PROSE
A25. A well known composer. ~ SLEEP
A27. Railway terminals. ~ RY
A28. This has three legs, is covered with scales and is inclined to be playful. ~ PIANO
A29. What she does with all mail marked private. ~ OPENS
A30. They say it with carnations. (If you do not get this, tune into the milky way.) ~ CONTENTMENT
A33. One good deed these fellows do, is to take tramps to the tall timbers. ~ SCOUTS
A35. Where the old Romans used to put their spectacles. ~ ARENA
A36. Common usage has made sausage of this. ~ SA
A37. Because they have been allowed to run wild, these are now red. (Oh dear! Oh dear!) ~ HARTS
A38. Exhibit "E" from Africa. Its American relative is apt to elope. ~ ELAND
A39. What the deposit for a rainy day turned out to be. ~ MUD
A40. If you are weary of this puzzle, here is a good place to stop (plur.). ~ INNS
A41. What Junior calls his sister and her girl friends. ~ SNIPS
A42. This reduces the cherry crop by pecks. ~ BILL
A43. A Honolulu special. ~ POI
A44. These have been in the cleaning business for years. ~ MATS
A45. Garage men tell us these cars make the best kind for a clean-up. ~ DUSTY
A46. A method used to reduce the waste line. ~ ECONOMY
A49. What Amos Keeter of New Jersey became after his bill passed the committee. ~ BITER
A50. President Roosevelt's biggest check. ~ SENATE
A51. Be careful! you might get in a jam here. ~ PANTRY
A52. Even these are getting stuck on movie stars. ~ EYELASHES

D1. The way a green driver is apt to turn when coming to a dangerous curve. ~ WHITE
D2. He who hesitates, does this. ~ HEMS
D3. The role mother's darling assumes when company arrives. ~ IMP
D4. If you want your ad to be read add this to it. ~ RE
D5. This makes a good line for a circus. ~ PARADE
D6. These are within the range of almost any family. ~ OVENS
D7. If you have a grain of horse sense you will get this. ~ OATS
D8. How she used to rattle on! ~ LIZ
D9. The kind of sensation the murder mystery created. ~ SPOOKY
D10. Last year this was a jig-saw puzzle, this year it is changed to roller skates. ~ CRAZE
D12. The way Uncle Sam has been in regard to war debts. ~ LENIENT
D13. Uncle Tom and Eliza. ~ SLAVES
D15. Much of this is seen at the beaches. ~ PERSONALITY
D16. The ship John Alden guided for miles. ~ COURTSHIP
D17. An American explorer. ~ BOONE
D19. Many people lost this at the Century of Progress on hot days. ~ PEP
D21. There will probably be a rush for these this fall. ~ FRATS
D24. One of these will give a pair of rose colored glasses. ~ PINTS
D25. What Congress will probably do to the American dollar. ~ SPEND
D26. Her reputation is leaping. ~ LENA
D28. These make a lip-stick decidedly out. ~ POUTS
D29. We will give you 4 tokens for these. ~ OMENS
D30. This is always running around the building. ~ CORNICE
D31. A catcher for the Cubs (plur.). ~ TRAPS
D32. The way the hero falls in love. ~ MADLY
D34. These should get a good paddling for being tipsy. ~ CANOES
D36. The kind of a day Arthur Mometer got a raise. ~ SULTRY
D38. A shiny coat. ~ ENAMEL
D39. A dough nut. ~ MISER
D41. What his conscience did to him for sitting up with a sick friend. ~ SMOTE
D42. Quarter cigars. ~ BUTTS
D45. What many speakers should do at a banquet. ~ DINE
D47. In spite of the depression, this was doubled at the end of 1932. ~ ONE
D48. What a horse says when it means no. ~ NAY
D49. What a lamb says when disgusted. ~ BAH
D51. An afterthought. ~ PS


